      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "for-each Loop"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "So far, we have been using the for loop to access each element of the array."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In Java, there is another variation of the for loop called the for-each loop. It makes accessing array elements really easy. Let's see an example."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "content": "1class Main {\n2    public static void main(String[] args) {\n3        \n4        // Create an array\n5        int numbers[] = {35, 42, 56, 62, 67};\n6        \n7        // Access each element using for each-loop \n8        for (int number : numbers) {\n9            System.out.println(number);\n10        }\n11    }\n12}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "35\n42\n56\n62\n67"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In the program,"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "for - syntax of the loop",
        "numbers - name of the array",
        "int number - temporary variable. You can name this variable anything you want."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The loop iterates 5 times because the array has 5 elements. In each iteration, the element of the array is assigned to the number variable. Hence, we get the element when we print number."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here's how the code works:"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Iteration",
